# 2. The seven roadmap decisions

## 2.1 Decision 1 — first slice

**Decision:** implement **S1 / Bundle A** first.

```text
RP-01  Production Object + immutable Production Revision identity/lifecycle
RP-03  minimum consumption closure + publish-readiness
```

Required product proof:

> A filmmaker can approve/publish one exact reusable production realization, close its declared consumption dependencies, retain it, inspect its provenance, and use its immutable identity later without depending on the process that originally created it.

Why this is first:

1. Every reusable Composition depends on exact Production Revision identity.
2. It closes the oldest P0 reusable-production orphan without introducing Composition or renderer complexity prematurely.
3. It directly establishes the principle needed for “build once, approve/freeze, reuse later.”
4. The dependency graph explicitly proves that a generalized representation registry is not required first.
5. It creates the narrowest source gate for distinguishing production identity, byte identity, and derivation identity.

## 2.2 Decision 2 — design-gate sequencing

The roadmap freezes gate order as follows:

```text
Before M11: G1
Before M12: G2
Before M13: G3 + G7
Before M14: G6, then remaining execution part of G7
Before M15: G4
Before M16: G5
Before M17A / first Performance milestone: G8 (Performance / Dialogue Co-Design)

Non-reordering side branches:
G9  targeted correction / delta realization after OB-03 exists
G10 profile-scoped qualification after RP-03 exists
G11 property-scoped reference evidence independent of reusable-production spine
```

Gate work may be researched early, but a later gate cannot silently redefine an earlier frozen production identity.

## 2.3 Decision 3 — source-gate boundaries

Source gates sit at product cut points rather than at arbitrary implementation-module boundaries:

```text
M11 gate  first publishable reusable Production Revision
M12 gate  stable occurrence identity and scoped composition editing
M13 gate  immutable authority-complete reusable world + history
M14 gate  production-world observation + pre-queue enforcement
M15 gate  compatibility-gated reusable revision evolution
M16 gate  intra-Shot event → explicit persistent consequence handoff
```

Each source gate must test its historical negative cases before broader UI or convenience behavior is accepted.

## 2.4 Decision 4 — REMEMBER THE WORLD completeness threshold

**REMEMBER THE WORLD closes at M13, not M12 and not M14.**

The threshold requires the dependency-complete equivalent of Bundle C:

```text
published Production Revisions
+ published Composition Revision
+ stable addressable occurrences
+ identity survival across Composition revisions
+ instance-bound world-state resolution
+ instance-bound spatial resolution
+ exact Composition↔Spatial binding
+ coherent Shot capture
+ historical reusable-production closure
```

Product claim after M13:

> SoloRing can remember which exact reusable world a Shot used, which occurrence was which, what persistent state applied, how that world was bound to spatial authority, and can reproduce the historical production graph without consulting current mutable state.

M13 does **not** claim that every executor can yet render this world.

## 2.5 Decision 5 — SHOOT THE WORLD completeness threshold

**SHOOT THE WORLD closes at M14.**

The threshold requires the dependency-complete equivalent of Bundle D:

```text
REMEMBER THE WORLD
+ generalized observation contract
+ production-world observation support
+ property-level preservation/trust contract
+ pre-queue capability negotiation
+ one declared production-capable retained representation/materialization path
```

Product claim after M14:

> A captured persistent reusable world can be translated into one supported production execution path, while any executor path unable to honor a hard captured requirement fails before queueing rather than silently weakening the Shot.

## 2.6 Decision 6 — parallel roots

The graph exposes eight independent roots, but the roadmap deliberately chooses **focus over parallel implementation** until SHOOT THE WORLD closes.

Before M14:

```text
IMPLEMENTATION PRIORITY
RP-01 reusable-production root only

ALLOWED IN PARALLEL
focused research/source-fit/design preparation for G5, G6, G8

DEFERRED IMPLEMENTATION
EV-01 intra-Shot events
PF-01 Performance Revision
RL-01 physical relationships
RV-03 QC
CW-01 candidate-world ingestion
VI-01 property-scoped reference evidence
```

Rationale:

- the reusable-world spine is the defining current product gap;
- parallel implementation would create more integration surfaces before the core production identity model stabilizes;
- the independent roots remain genuinely independent and therefore lose no architectural validity by waiting;
- design preparation can proceed without allowing those branches to shape M11–M13 authority prematurely.

After M14, the roadmap prioritizes safe world evolution and persistent consequences before optional candidate-producer expansion.


Additional allowed side-branch design after M11 publication capability exists:

```text
G9 targeted correction/delta-realization design
G10 qualification contract design
G11 property-scoped reference-claim design
```

Neither branch may delay M12/M13 merely to complete a generalized qualification or reference platform. Qualification becomes a hard execution gate only for a realization policy that explicitly requires applicable evidence.

## 2.7 Decision 7 — deliberate exclusions

The first reusable-world program deliberately excludes capabilities that are not required to prove the two core completeness thresholds.

Excluded from M11–M14 unless a source gate proves a specific hard need:

```text
RP-02 generalized multi-representation registry
RP-04 broad derivation framework beyond minimum provenance required for publication
RP-06 general fork operation
CO-04 bulk/procedural population and promotion
RV-02 generated-detail promotion
PF-01/PF-02 Performance Revision
RL-01/RL-02 physical relationships/contact
CW-01/CW-02/CW-03 candidate-world ingestion
simulation/physical prediction producer integration
universal rig/deformation system
continuous story-clock domain
editorial/finishing domains
multi-user collaboration
agent-driven production workflows
```

A capability may enter an earlier milestone only if its owning milestone source-fit demonstrates that the declared product slice is otherwise impossible to close. Convenience is not enough.

