# 11. Product-completeness ladder

The roadmap defines explicit product checkpoints so later work cannot overclaim completion.

| Checkpoint | Milestone | Product claim |
|---|---|---|
| **Reusable Object** | M11 | One approved production realization can be published, retained, and reused by immutable identity. |
| **Reusable Set** | M12 | Exact revisions can be assembled with stable addressable occurrence identity and explicit edit scope. |
| **REMEMBER THE WORLD** | M13 | Persistent world/instance/state/spatial/binding facts are coherently captured and historically reproducible. |
| **SHOOT THE WORLD** | M14 | The captured reusable world constrains a supported execution path with pre-queue hard-capability enforcement. |
| **EVOLVE THE WORLD** | M15 | New reusable revisions can enter current work through explicit compatibility without rewriting history. |
| **CHANGE DURING THE SHOT** | M16 | Intra-Shot state changes can hand off explicit persistent consequences into downstream continuity. |

These claims are cumulative.

A separate non-cumulative readiness checkpoint may exist alongside the ladder:

| Side checkpoint | Earliest basis | Product claim |
|---|---|---|
| **QUALIFIED REALIZATION** | M11 + QL-01/QL-02 + stable realization profile | One exact Production Revision has retained evidence that it satisfies a declared qualification protocol for one exact realization profile. |

`QUALIFIED REALIZATION` does not replace publication, approval, REMEMBER THE WORLD, or SHOOT THE WORLD. It becomes mandatory only when a production-use policy requires it.

M14 cannot close if M13 is incomplete. M15 cannot claim safe evolution if compatibility is only advisory. M16 cannot claim persistent consequences if Take approval still creates state implicitly.

