# 16. Roadmap risks and mitigations

## 16.1 Risk — M11 becomes a universal asset platform

Mitigation: freeze G1 around one declared downstream consumption path; explicitly retain the RP-02 non-edge.

## 16.2 Risk — M12 creates scene-file identity instead of production identity

Mitigation: source-gate occurrence identity after representation/file renames and hierarchy changes; identity must remain stable.

## 16.3 Risk — M13 duplicates spatial authority

Mitigation: G3 must define binding, not a second transform winner. Conflicting authority blocks readiness.

## 16.4 Risk — G3 bindability remains a vague phrase

Mitigation: G3.1 is a mandatory plan-freeze question. M13 cannot freeze while eligibility is implicit.

## 16.5 Risk — historical closure postponed until M14

Mitigation: HI-01 is M13 scope and part of REMEMBER THE WORLD. Renderer work cannot be used to justify incomplete historical production memory.

## 16.6 Risk — M14 becomes a wrapper with no new-domain execution

Mitigation: M14A and M14B are distinct. Only OB-02 plus one real production-world representation path closes SHOOT THE WORLD.

## 16.7 Risk — compatibility treated as “same object means safe”

Mitigation: M15 requires explicit consumer-specific verdicts and versioned translations where needed.

## 16.8 Risk — Take approval starts mutating world state

Mitigation: M16 keeps Take approval and persistent consequence adoption separate product actions.

## 16.9 Risk — independent roots distract from core world loop

Mitigation: M14 is now closed; future parallelism must still be justified by product evidence and explicit authorization rather than by an independent-root label alone.

## 16.10 Risk — roadmap milestone number becomes authorization

Mitigation: milestone numbering here expresses dependency order only. Every milestone requires a separately reviewed/frozen implementation plan and explicit implementation authorization.

